.TH "Pacboy" "1" "April 2026" "Pacboy User Manuals"
.SH NAME
pacboy \- Custom paquet manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
pacboy <\f[I]library name\f[R]> [\f[I]options\f[R]] pacboy
<\f[I]options\f[R]>
.PP
See \f[B]OPTIONS\f[R] below
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pacboy is a small derivative of pacman, to manage local custom
libraries.
It is written in C and functions by copying the files it finds at the
location into the destination
.PP
Pacboy has 2 modes : installation mode, which is triggered when no other
modes are found, requiring the \f[B]first argument\f[R] being the
library that will be included, and the \f[B]second argument\f[R] being
the path where it is installed.
The other modes are triggered by the various command options :
\f[CR]\-\-generate\-config\f[R], that will generate a configuration
file, \f[CR]\-D\f[R] or \f[CR]\-I\f[R] for Database and Information
modes, \f[CR]\-\-change\-config\f[R], to update pacboy\(cqs
configuration, \f[CR]\-P\f[R] pull changes from database, and
\f[CR]\-\-help\f[R] or \f[CR]\-h\f[R] for help, as well as
\f[CR]\-v\f[R] for version, also \f[CR]\-u\f[R] to check if a newer
version is available
.PP
The order of detecting modes is :
.IP "1." 3
Help, through : \f[CR]\-h\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-help\f[R]
.IP "2." 3
Version, with : \f[CR]\-v\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
Check for update : \f[CR]\-u\f[R]
.IP "4." 3
Database : \f[CR]\-D\f[R]
.IP "5." 3
Config mode : \f[CR]\-\-generate\-config\f[R]
.IP "6." 3
Information mode : \f[CR]\-I\f[R]
.IP "7." 3
Change config : \f[CR]\-\-change\-config\f[R]
.IP "8." 3
Pull database : \f[CR]\-P\f[R]
.IP "9." 3
Default is installation mode
.SS HELP
Help mode will display help page, no option can alter the behavior of
this mode.
.SS VERSION
Version mode will display the version in standard output and exits
.SS UPDATE CHECK
Update check will fetch from remote address
(https://git.greensky.tf/Greensky/pacboy), and will see if a newer
version is available from code.
Curl is required, since libcurl is used to make the fetch
.PP
It returns the new version number if available, or says \f[I]Up to
date\f[R] if no newer version is found
.SS DATABASE
In Database mode, it will simply list all the registered libraries.
No option can modify the behavior of this mode
.SS CONFIGURATION
In configuration mode, pacboy will generate a configuration file, with
the given function names.
You must give the function names in comma\-separated list, using the
\f[CR]\-\-deps\f[R] option, and you can redirect the output from
\f[I]stdout\f[R] to \f[CR]/path/to/dir/paquet.boy\f[R] by specifying
\f[CR]/path/to/dir\f[R] in the \f[CR]\-od\f[R] option (standing for
\f[B]output directory\f[R]).
Pacboy will automatically add the \f[CR]/paquet.boy\f[R] at the end of
the path.
.PP
In order to specify the \f[B]features\f[R] of the paquet, you need to
use \f[CR]\-\-features\f[R] in a comma\-separated list of values.
If some features require specific functions, specify them inside
brackets ( \f[CR][]\f[R] ), in a comma\-separated list of values.
For more informations about features, refer the to the
\f[B]FEATURES\f[R] section
.IP
.EX
pacboy \-\-generate\-config \-od \(ti/paclibs/args \-\-deps \(dqstreq,parse_int\(dq
pacboy \-\-generate\-config \-\-deps \(dqstreq\(dq
pacboy \-od \(dq./paclibs/string\-chained\-list\(dq \-\-deps \(dqstreq\(dq \-\-generate\-config
pacboy \-\-generate\-config \-\-deps \(dqstreq\(dq \-\-features \(dqclone,comparison[ordcompare,eqcompare]\(dq
.EE
.PP
Pacboy can have configuration files for its libraries.
It contains the dependencies.
In pacboy, depencies are functions that the library expected in its C or
H files, assumed it existed but never defined.
The names inside the configuration file, below the \f[CR][deps]\f[R] are
the functions the library is assuming are defined.
An example of configuration file (\f[I]paquet.boy\f[R]) can be this :
.IP
.EX
 [deps]
 streq
 disassemble_array
 [features]
 sync
 async
  \- thread
  \- process
.EE
.PP
In this example, the library is expecting the user to give the according
H files for the function declaration.
The \f[CR][features]\f[R] part concerns \f[B]FEATURES\f[R]
.SS INFORMATION
In information mode, Pacboy will show the informations of the given
library, such informations being :
.IP \(bu 2
The name
.IP \(bu 2
The location
.IP \(bu 2
If it has any, the list of expected functions
.IP \(bu 2
An example of a command that could be used to install it
.SS CHANGE CONFIG
In Change config, pacboy will prompt the user for successive parameters,
to answer in the terminal.
Leaving a field blank doesn\(cqt change it.
Fields that can be modified :
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]repo_url\f[R] : The url of the repo.
More on that in \f[B]PULL\f[R]
.SS PULL
In pull mode, pacboy will execute a succession of commands, being :
.IP "1." 3
Remove local packages
.IP "2." 3
Cloning the configured database :
\f[CR]git clone \-b mirror \-\-only\-branch <configured url> <path of libs>\f[R]
.IP "3." 3
Removing the \f[CR].git\f[R] folder
.PP
Note the \f[B]\-b mirror\f[R] that specifies the mirror branch only.
This is because the database must contain exclusively the folders for
the libraries, and nothing else, so a dedicated branch should be set up.
.SS INSTALLATION
In installation mode, Pacboy looks for a folder in
\f[CR]\(ti/.local/share/paclibs\f[R], given as the first argument of the
command.
It then reads the configuration file it it exists to try to find the
required functions declaration files.
More on this below.
If not all the functions are found, it will throw an error.
When all the headers file are given through the \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R]
parameter, in the format of :
\f[CR]function_name=header_file.h,function_name2=header_file2.h\f[R], it
will copy\-paste the names in the includes.
Since it is custom libraries and custom usage, it is up to the user to
make sure the paths are correct, and valid in relative pathing.
.IP
.EX
pacboy make ./
pacboy string\-chained\-list ./src/structs \-\-include \(dqstreq=../aux/tools.h\(dq
pacboy pyassembler ./src/assemblers/ \-\-include \(dq*=../aux/tools.h\(dq
pacboy args ./src/aux \-\-include \(dqstreq=../aux/tools/string.h,parse_int=../aux/parsers/int.h\(dq
.EE
.SH FEATURES
Pacboy offers a features system, to filter the needs.
.PP
Basically, a feature is a folder in a library.
For instance, a library having \(lqtool.c\(rq at the root, and
\(lqaux\(rq as a folder at root, \(lqaux\(rq is considered a feature.
Every folder at root is considered a feature, and every feature is a
folder at root, so, for instance, in \f[CR]./aux/parsers/\f[R],
\f[B]parsers\f[R] is not considered a feature
.PP
A feature can have specific dependencies.
In order to generate a config file with a feature needing dependencies,
use the \f[CR]\-\-features\f[R] flag, followed by something like :
\f[CR]\(dqfeature_name[dependency_1,dependency_2,...]\f[R].
If a feature required no dependency, don\(cqt pass the brackets.
.PP
To include features in installation, use the \f[CR]\-\-features\f[R]
flag.
It will automatically add the dependencies of the feature in the
required dependencies needed to be passed in \f[CR]\-\-include\f[R]
.SH OPTIONS
\-h, \-\-help : Display help page
.PP
\-D : Enters database mode.
It just shows the list of available libraries.
.PP
\-od \f[I]path to directory\f[R] : Redirect the output of
\f[B]CONFIGURATION MODE\f[R] to a file named \f[CR]paquet.boy\f[R] in
the given directory.
This option only has effect in configuration mode.
.PP
\-I : Enters information mode.
The name of the library must be specified as the first argument of the
program
.PP
\-\-generate\-config : Enter config mode.
This option must go along with the \f[B]\-\-deps\f[R] option, in the
form of comma\-separated function names that are expected to be defined
by the library.
.PP
\-\-include \f[I]comma\-separated pairs of function\-names/header
files\f[R] : Specify the header files in installation mode that the
library requires externally declared functions.
The paths must be valid in relative pathing.
.PP
\-\-deps \f[I]comma\-separeted function names\f[R] : Specify the names
of the required libraries for \f[B]CONFIGRATION MODE\f[R]
.PP
\-\-change\-config : Enter the configuration mode
.PP
\-P : Pull the database from git.
.PP
\-\-features : Specify the features, for either \f[B]INSTALLATION
MODE\f[R], or \f[B]CONFIGURATION MODE\f[R].
Refer to the according part of the manual for more information
.PP
\-v : Displays the current version and exits
.PP
\-u : Check for newer versions
.SH RETURN VALUE
The command returns 0 if everything went alright, 1 otherwise
